  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "Why compile, not retrieve",
      "items": [
        "RAG searches the sources again on every question, so nothing learned on one question helps the next.",
        "This KB reads each source once, in full, and folds its facts into pages that already exist, with cross-references and a provenance trail.",
        "The payoff compounds: the tenth source lands into a wiki that already knows the first nine. The cost is that compilation is judgment work, so ingest keeps a human in the session."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "violet",
      "title": "Three layers, and who writes where",
      "items": [
        "raw/ is yours and immutable: the agent reads it and never edits or deletes it.",
        "wiki/ is the agent's: pages, the index.md catalog and the append-only log.md. You read it in Obsidian and rarely touch it; a hand edit is committed separately as yours before the agent's own work.",
        "The schema (AGENTS.md) is co-owned and says how the wiki is structured and which workflow runs when; per-instance settings live in kb.toml so the tooling stays byte-identical across instances.",
        "The boundary is what makes a wide edit safe: fifteen pages can be rewritten in one pass because the sources underneath cannot be damaged."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "emerald",
      "title": "The write path: a source becomes pages",
      "items": [
        "Capture is mechanical and separate from ingest: /research, /scout, /transcript and /snippet land dated files with provenance frontmatter in raw/ and stop. staging/ingest.md queues what is not yet ingested, with a watch-out per source.",
        "Ingest is one source per session: read it in full, discuss takeaways, create or update every page it touches, add each new page to index.md, append an ingest entry to log.md.",
        "Contestable assertions go in [!claim] callouts with confidence, status and an evidence line citing the raw file, so a later source can contest or supersede them in place.",
        "A source that bears on how the KB itself works is logged as an implication and handled as a decision, not changed mid-ingest."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "amber",
      "title": "The read path: questions and the MCP server",
      "items": [
        "A question starts at index.md, one line per page, so the agent routes to the few pages that matter before opening any of them.",
        "The answer comes back in conversation with wikilink citations; it is filed as a syntheses/ page only when you ask.",
        "Software reads the KB the same way through mcp/server.py: search_kb, read_page, get_index. Writes stay interactive by design."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "rose",
      "title": "Keeping it honest: claims, lint, the judgment pass",
      "items": [
        "scripts/lint.py is the deterministic half: every page indexed once, every wikilink resolving, every raw: path existing, every claim callout well-formed with a status from kb.toml, raw dates consistent, orphans and unpaged terms flagged.",
        "The judgment half is what the script cannot do: contradictions between pages, claims a newer source supersedes, inference stated as fact, missing cross-references. Both end in a lint log entry.",
        "Decisions close the loop on the rules: a schema implication becomes a proposed ADR under decisions/, and an accepted one changes AGENTS.md or the tooling on a branch with review."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "slate",
      "title": "Why it's built this way",
      "items": [
        "One source per session: ingest is judgment-heavy; a batch would move the gate from the conversation to a review queue (ADR-012 records why nothing runs on a timer).",
        "Capture never touches the wiki: a fetched page can be wrong or hostile without reaching a wiki page; text inside a source is data, never an instruction.",
        "Claims are callouts, not prose: a callout can be linted for shape and superseded without being overwritten (ADR-010, ADR-007).",
        "Config in kb.toml, never in code: that is what lets /sync-upstream port template updates and lets a child KB stay a standalone folder (ADR-003).",
        "The MCP server is read-only: ingestion stays an interactive workflow, never an API call (ADR-004).",
        "Content lands on main, structure goes through review: one commit per workflow run; schema, tooling and skill changes take a branch and a PR citing the source (ADR-014)."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
